00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.490 align:start position:0%
 
Joey hunt very good morning to you  thanks so much for talking to us here on  Sky news this morning so Britain faces  its biggest hit to living standards on  record wiping out eight years of  improvements the economy is already in  recession and now people are facing a  huge rise in the tax burden you've been  in power for 12 years it's a pretty  bleak Legacy from the conservatives  isn't it  well what we have is a very challenging  International situation the dropping  growth in Germany for example is even  higher than here countries all over the  world are dealing with the inflation  shot caused by the Ukraine crisis and  what we have to do as a government is to  say what is the big thing that we can do  to help families to help businesses the  most and what people are worried about  is the rise in the cost of living that  Weekly shop going up their mortgage  rates going up their energy Bills going  up and this is a plan which when you cut  through the difficult decisions that I  announced yesterday it brings inflation  right down you start to see growth going  right up and you start to see our  economy getting back onto an even Keel  and that's the most important thing as a  conservative I know sometimes you have  to take difficult decisions but if in  the end that means more stability for  families and we're protecting the NHS  and schools as you said then we know  we're doing the right thing for the long  term and that's what we do in these  kinds situations would you say you say  that it's a challenging International  situation you're blaming Global factors  and undoubtedly the pandemic and the war  in Ukraine have had an impact but don't  the conservatives need to take some  responsibility as well I mean a lot of  what you're doing is about undoing the  mess that the mini budget made and also  taking steps to try to convince the  markets that you're trustworthy  well we reverse those measures in the  miniature budget in three weeks so I  think we acted very quickly we've been  completely open yes there were some  mistakes but when you look at America  that's increased taxes by 800 billion  dollars you look at uh Germany and  France that are now saying they're going  to reduce their borrowing and introduce  much stricter rules you look at what  Putin is doing in Ukraine this is an  international situation coming straight  after a once in a lifetime pandemic so  these are very serious issues sorry to  interrupt Jeremy Hunt but we have the  lowest growth in the G7 why is that  well that's not true we actually have  the highest growth in the G7 this year  and if you look at the last 12 years  we've actually had the third highest  growth in the G7 despite a lot of global  turbulence now over the next two years  it is going to be challenging but I  think people want a government that is  taking difficult decisions has a plan  that will bring down inflation stop  those big rises in the the cost of  energy bills in the weekly shop and at  the same time it's taking measures to  help people get through this difficult  period the support for energy bills will  mean that the average house will have  500 pounds less to pay in their energy  bills we've increased the national  living wage which will mean an extra  sixteen hundred pounds in the pay  packets of people who work full-time and  indeed protecting the triple lot for  pensioners so we're doing absolutely  everything we can to help people whilst  tackling this this very very challenging  International situation  but you're part of the same government  that said we were going to see the  biggest tax cuts in 50 years a 45  billion pounds worth just eight weeks  ago now you're announcing huge tax Rises  and spending cuts as well worth a total  of 55 billion pounds what's the public  supposed to make of this total reversal  in Direction in just a short space of  time what does it do to the conservative  party's credibility  well we've been honest with people that  there were some mistakes made but we're  also saying there are much bigger issues  to solve than what happened in the mini  budget we've got a big challenge because  of the costs of covid the costs of the  war in Ukraine and what we announced  yesterday was a long-term plan to see us  through to the other side and I think  people want a government that is  prepared to take difficult decisions to  write the economy to protect jobs  yesterday the office for Budget  responsibility said the measures I  announced will save 70 000 jobs they'll  reduce the severity of the recession by  about a third so that is a government  that is doing everything it can to help  families get through what is a very very  challenging period  will you say help families through a  challenging period but disposable income  is going to fall by more than seven  percent over the next two years that's  the biggest fall on record we're all  going to be a lot worse off aren't we  that's what happens when you have high  inflation and that's what happens when  Putin is able to Blackmail us and  countries all over the world by forcing  a big rise in international gas prices  and that's why one of the things we  announced yesterday was a new nuclear  power plant the first state-backed  nuclear power plant for 30 years to try  and stop people like Putin being able to  do that blackmail which is so damaging  to our living standards I think it's  important Anna to recognize as a country  we've had bigger challenges before  and we've always thrived and prospered  because people have been able to or been  willing to take tough difficult  decisions when they're called for  because people know in their hearts  there are big big issues that we have to  resolve and they want a government  that's prepared to take those decisions  people will also ask how they're going  to pay their bills I mean we're facing  the worst two years of modern history  for household incomes millions are going  to be dragged into higher tax bans  council tax is likely to go up energy  bills up again in the spring and they're  already  eye-wateringly high the resolution  Foundation says the typical annual tax  bill Will Rise by 1700 pounds how are  people going to afford it  well that's why we announced a lot of  things yesterday let me tell you some of  the things that we announced that will  help people first of all the cap on  energy bills at 3 000 pounds will save  the average house 500 pounds uh the  increase in the state pension by a  record 10.1 percent uh will increase the  average pensioners income by 870 pounds  then you've got the increase in the  National living wage which we talked  about up to sixteen hundred pounds then  we're doing one-off payments to people  on benefits of 900 pounds one-off  payments to pensioners of 300 pounds on  our payments to people on with  disabilities to of 150 pounds and then  you've got the reversal of the Health  and Social care Levy which is again  another nearly 400 pounds so you know  put those together and for the most  vulnerable people there are thousands  and thousands of pounds of help that  we're offering but we're doing more than  that we're saying what is the long-term  solution to these cost of living  pressures it's to bring down inflation  and this is a plan that sees inflation  fall markedly in the second half of next  year and that in the end is going to be  the thing that deals with people's the  root cause of people's worries and  that's what we've tried to do today  one of the things you mentioned there  and that was honoring the triple lock  seeing pensions rise with inflation is  it fair though that wealthy retirees  should enjoy that rise in their income  when public sector workers can't  well I think the truth is first of all  pensioners have retired they don't have  the ability to work more or work longer  hours in the way that uh people of  working age do but I think it's wrong to  say that only the poorest pensioners are  feeling the squeeze at the moment I  think this is something that's affecting  everyone and I think it's right having  made that promise to pensioners in our  Manifesto that we would have this triple  lock I think this is exactly the kind of  tough time that people want it to kick  in and so that's why I think it's the  right thing to do but when a lot of  people say you are protecting pensioners  but it's workers that are really being  clobbered aren't they given the tax  Rises that are being imposed on them  does it pay to work under your plans  well we've just increased the national  living wage to 10 pounds 42 that's  nearly 10 increase and that's precisely  to make sure we do reward people for  working and support people who are on  Lower pay so I think we're very aware of  those challenges we're not pretending  that this isn't going to be a difficult  time for everyone but what we have is a  plan we're protecting the NHS and  schools as we get through a difficult  period And when we get to the other side  you can see inflation coming right down  growth going right up unemployment  coming down all the things that you know  their economic statistics but they  actually affect ordinary families up and  down the country they affect how much we  pay for the things we buy the effect the  security of our jobs and that's why our  first responsibility has to be to take  the difficult decisions that sort out  the root problems the economy is facing  the tax Rises you've announced are  astonishing as a share of GDP that  they're at their highest level since the  second world war and there are many in  your own party that are deeply  uncomfortable about that about putting  up taxes when we're in recession they  say it's not very conservative  well the office of budget  responsibilities say yesterday that what  we're doing is actually making the  recession shallower it's saving jobs  um but what I would say to my  conservative colleagues is you know  there is nothing conservative about  spending money that you haven't got  there is nothing conservative about not  tackling inflation there's nothing  conservative about ducking difficult  decisions that put the economy on track  and we've done all of those things and  that's why this is a very conservative  package to make sure that we sort out  the economy which is one of the most  important things that people expect from  us as a party but they might say back to  you household incomes Falling by seven  percent in the next 18 months  essentially the run-up to the next  general election taxation as a share of  GDP at its highest level since the war  unemployment is going to be rising house  prices are going to be rising why would  you vote conservative at the next  election  and I'm gonna have to make this the last  answer I'm afraid because I've got to do  some other interviews but let me answer  that very directly people vote  conservative because they know we will  take the tough and difficult decisions  in the long-term interests of the  country that's what we are doing today  none of this is easy but it's the right  thing to do and if we're going to bring  down the cost of people's shopping bills  make people's jobs more secure look  after the economy then people know  that's what they get with the  conservative party at the same time as  that we're making sure that the NHS has  the support it needs our schools have  the support they need to get them  through a very difficult period and  that's why I think it's a balanced  package that everyone will feel able to  support thank you very much okay Jeremy  Hunt thank you we'll let you go and do  those other interviews thanks very much  indeed

